    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d438">

                <pb facs="0551" n="541" sameAs="frus:frus1948v07/pg541"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01/11–548: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Canton</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Ludden</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Canton</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-11-05T17:00:00+08:00">November 5, 1948—5
                            p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 6—1:54 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>231. I have been informed by American source close to T. V. Soong<anchor n="34"
                        xml:id="d438-f1"/> that on November 4 Governor refused accept position as
                    Premier unless “more realistic” attitude could be adopted toward current
                    military and economic problems. My informant inferred that Soong’s refusal
                    yesterday was culmination of recent strong pressure on him from Generalissimo to
                    accept post. Sent Nanking 253; repeated Department 231.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ludden</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d438-f1" n="34" xml:id="d438-f1-content">
                <p>Governor of Kwangtung.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
